THINK = {
1: r"Before any clever model, remember that a computer sees only numbers, so your first task is translation: rows are things, columns are measurements. Time spent checking the table (missing values, odd units, duplicates) is the best investment in machine learning, because a model will happily learn from mistakes.",
2: r"Exploring data is a conversation: ask a small question, look at the answer, ask the next one. Charts are arguments, so make them honest (start axes sensibly, compare like with like). Whenever a summary surprises you, split the data by a hidden group and see whether the story changes.",
3: r"The one question machine learning must answer is ``will this work on data it has not seen?'' To estimate that honestly you hide some data from the model, as a teacher hides the exam questions. Every trick of the chapter (splits, cross-validation, metrics) protects that hidden exam from leaking into practice.",
4: r"A linear model is the simplest honest guess: the output is a weighted sum of the inputs. Training chooses the weights; regularisation says ``prefer small weights unless the data insist,'' which fights overfitting. If a straight line is not enough, bend the inputs (feature maps) instead of abandoning the line.",
5: r"A tree plays twenty questions with your data, choosing at each step the question that best separates the answers. One tree memorises; many trees that disagree, averaged together, generalise. Boosting takes a different route: fix the previous model's mistakes one small tree at a time.",
6: r"With no answers to learn from, we look for shape: which points sit close together, and which directions carry the most variation. The danger is finding patterns that are not there, so always ask whether the structure survives rescaling, a different seed, and a comparison with random noise.",
7: r"A neural network is a stack of simple steps, each a linear map followed by a bend. Training is the same downhill walk as before, and backpropagation is simply the chain rule organised so that one backward pass gives every weight's slope. Once you have done a tiny network by hand, big ones are the same idea with more bookkeeping.",
8: r"Training a large network is less about clever mathematics than about keeping the downhill walk stable: steps neither too large nor too small, a sensible starting point, and ways to stop the signal from exploding or fading. When training misbehaves, change one thing at a time and watch the loss curve like a doctor watches a pulse.",
9: r"Images have a structure that a generic network ignores: nearby pixels belong together and the same pattern can appear anywhere. A convolution builds that assumption in by reusing a small detector everywhere. Good design in machine learning is often exactly this: put what you know about the problem into the shape of the model.",
10: r"For sequences, the past matters, so the model needs a memory. An RNN carries a running summary forward, but squeezing everything into one summary forgets early information. Gates in an LSTM are a learned way to decide what to keep, which is the idea attention later improves on.",
11: r"Attention replaces ``pass the message along a chain'' with ``let every word look directly at every other word and decide whom to listen to.'' Query, key and value are just three roles in that lookup. Seeing it as a soft dictionary lookup makes the maths feel small.",
12: r"Instead of predicting a label, a generative model learns to create. Each family (autoregressive, VAE, GAN, diffusion) is a different answer to one question: how do I turn simple randomness into realistic data? Judge a model by what it makes, and be sceptical of any single number that claims to measure quality.",
13: r"In reinforcement learning nobody tells you the right action; you learn from consequences, and your choices change what you see next. The central tension is exploration (try new things) against exploitation (use what works). The Bellman equation says a state's value is the reward now plus the value of where you land.",
14: r"A model in production is a living system: its data change, its users change, and silent failures are common. Treat deployment as a measurement problem. Decide in advance what you will log, what counts as drift, and what you will do when an alarm fires.",
15: r"When there is no single correct answer, evaluation becomes the hard part. Each method (reference metrics, human ratings, a judge model) has its own bias, so use more than one, and measure the bias. The golden question is always: could a bad system score well on this test?",
16: r"An agent is a language model in a loop with tools. More autonomy means more power and more ways to fail, so use the simplest design that works: a fixed workflow when the steps are known, an agent only when the path really is uncertain. Judge it by what it did, step by step, not just the final sentence.",
17: r"A paper is an argument, and a reproduction is how you check it. Read in passes: what is the claim, how is it supported, can I rebuild the core? Keep notes so that failure is informative: a gap between your number and theirs is a clue, not an embarrassment.",
18: r"The capstone is practice for the real job: a model that can be called, a test that can be rerun, and a claim you checked yourself. If you can say honestly what your system proves and what it does not, you have learned the most important lesson in the book.",
}
